o you get this kind of  thinking going well I wanted his social  approval so now as I go to interact with  him in the future what kinds of things  am I gonna want to be telling him I'm  gonna want to tell him hey I think I  made a mistake why because he's gonna  give me a pellet for that in the  form of engagement in the form of saying  wow that's really great that you  admitted that I think that's amazing  here's my perspective and as I'm open  minded to us his perspective as I'm not  swatting that away he's giving me all of  this reinforcement for that what is he  not reinforcing confirmatory style  thought and so now it's like you get to  have your cake and eat it too right you  get to be an exploratory thinker but  also still feel really good about  yourself I also feel like you have a  positive narrative because you've you've  changed the way that I like to put it  maybe because I'm a poker players you've  changed the definition of what it means  to win is there a politics or an  ideology embedded in the way you're  talking about stuff because one of the  things in contemporary America now we  talk a lot about echo chambers and that  you know partly and it's good overall  but you know people people can retreat  to vast endless storehouses of people  who will confirm everything that you  already believe whether it's MSNBC or  Fox News or you know the New York Times  versus the Washington Times or something  like that and what you're talking about  it strikes me as very much of a kind of  enlightenment for you of let the truth  you know we're never going to get there  so in a way it's kind of a postmodern  enlightenment we're not we're never  going to know all the facts we're never  going to know everything but we can  approximate it by you know swearing  allegiance to finding out what is true  rather than what is convenient and it  sounded a lot to me in reading your book  some of it like Friedrich Hayek's  thought which really and Karl Popper  emphasizing the limits of knowledge and  then how do you get a little bit smarter  and how are you disinterested so that  you are actually you know moving forward  and and getting closer to truth or  progress is that am i off there or is is  there an ad you have an ideology or a  politics behind all of this I would say  that for me it's more of the politics of  constructive dissent so take your choice  but what I think is that these echo  chambers that we get whether it's the  echo chamber of you know Democrats or  the echo chamber of Republicans you know  where it's all about yes yes yes we're  right and the other side is bad I don't  think it's good for our society I don't  think it's good for our palate  for sure I don't think it bodes well for  the state of the union so if I think  that regardless of whether you lean  democrat you lean Republican you lean  classic liberal whatever it might be  whatever your party identity is let's  have it not be the overriding identity  so that you're willing to override your  principles override the fact of the  matter close your mind off to what the  other side has to say I kind of go back  to again I go back to John Stuart Mill a  lot on this shot through the book he is  shot through the book because I would  like everybody to read on Liberty please  there you go  please everybody go read that book read  it before mine or after I guess I should  say read it after mind of what by them  together by them together a damn right  so um so here's the thing when we've  descended into an echo chamber of only  listening to the opinions that affirm us  because we are so sure that we are right  what happens in that situation is that  whatever your truth is has atrophied  like even if the even if that is  actually the objective truth your  inability to be able to defend it  against somebody who has a rationale and  we I'm not talking about people who are  just like yelling you're dumb right but  people who have a rational reasonable  explanation for why they hold an  opposing viewpoint you need to be able  to debate that effectively I think so  and and this is John Stuart Mill says  it's even if something is a hundred  percent true you better be able to  defend that against somebody who  believes something differently so for  example I happen to believe the earth is  round but if I can't debate that  effectively against a flat earther it's  somewhat meaningless that I believe that  the earth is round and when we descend  into these political echo chambers I  think that we're descending into that  problem sure okay  the earth is round but you can no longer  defend it against the flat earthers so  what does it mean that that you believe  that anymore  I think it completely loses its meaning  and I would argue that one of the things  that happens when you force yourself to  be able to defend it against the  rational actor  who hold different opinions is that you  very often find out that your belief  needs a little bit of calibration and  that's good for all of us right I mean  when I say oh you disagree with me if my  identity is no longer about being right  but it's about being someone who's  searching for the most accurate  objective truth of the matter because  you disagree with me I no longer view  you as an enemy I view as someone who  can really help me even though we  disagree you can now be really helpful  either because you moderate my opinion  or because you make me better at  understanding why I have that opinion in  the first place and either of those is  good well we will leave it there thank  you so much we've been talking with  Annie Duke she's the author most  recently of thinking in bets making  smarter decisions when you don't have  all the facts thanks so much thank you  for reason I'm Nikolas be  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

